0,Goreng Goreng - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goreng_Goreng,"The Gureng Gureng were divided into several clans, such as the Wakgun. Traditional lore was transmitted at a djaparlagin or a ""singing corroboree"".[9].Aboriginal Tribes of Australia. Australian National University Press. Archived from the original on 2 February 2021.",Gureng Gureng,Australian
1,Taribelang language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taribelang_language,"Taribelang, also known as Bunda and Gureng-Gureng, is a language of Queensland. Although no longer spoken as a native language by the Taribelang or Bunda people, it is spoken as a 2nd or 3rd language by under 100.",Gureng Gureng,Australian
2,Always Will Be - Indigenous Australian Culture & Music,https://www.alwayswillbe.com/,"Gureng Gureng. Baradha.Discover Deadly Music. Explore Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander musicians sharing their stories, culture, and connection to Country through music. Explore 87 artists and hundreds of songs.",Gureng Gureng,Australian
3,Gureng-gureng Meaning | Goong.com - New Generation Dictionary,https://goong.com/word/gurenggureng-meaning/,"During our visit to Australia, we learned about the Gureng-gureng traditions that highlight the importance of nature. The Gureng-gureng language is part of a broader family of Indigenous languages spoken in the region. Cultural ceremonies held by the Gureng-gureng community often involve music and dance that have been passed down for generations.",Gureng Gureng,Australian
4,Australharmony - A checklist of colonial era musical transcriptions of...,https://www.sydney.edu.au/paradisec/australharmony/checklist-indigenous-music-1.php,"Checklist is Australian sheet music prints 1835-c.1850. Music and instruments for sale. Organisations, venues, events, &c., register A-Z. Register of British military bands in Australia.Gureng-Gureng, Three Moon Creek, Upper Burnett River, QLD.",Gureng Gureng,Australian
5,Australia’s female First Nations rappers turn pain into power | Music,https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/dec/30/australia-female-first-nations-indigenous-rappers-rap-hip-hop-music,"In far north Queensland, Dizzy, a Takalak, Agwamin, Gureng Gureng and Wokka Wokka woman, grew up listening to jazz and blues. At 16 she released her first rap song, No Shame – a message about not letting that feeling hold you back.",Gureng Gureng,Australian
